Capturing variables — effectively final.

A lambda isn't sealed off from its surroundings — it can use variables from the enclosing scope. That's what makes callbacks useful: the lambda remembers the values around it when it was created. A lambda that does this is called a closure — it closes over those variables. But Java places one firm rule on what it may capture.

The rule is this: a lambda may read a local variable from the enclosing method only if that variable is final, or effectively final — meaning it's never reassigned after it's initialized. So, int factor equals three, never reassigned, is effectively final; and a function, n, arrow, n times factor, happily captures it, giving thirty for an input of ten. But if you write int count equals zero, then a runnable that prints count, and then reassign count to one — now count isn't effectively final any more, and the lambda won't compile. You don't have to write the word final; you just have to treat the variable as final. Reassign a captured local anywhere, and the lambda stops compiling.

Why does this restriction exist? Because a lambda can outlive the method that created it — stored in a field, returned, or run later on another thread. So Java captures the variable's value at creation time, not a live link to the stack slot, which may be long gone by the time the lambda actually runs. Requiring effective-finality guarantees there's no confusion between "the value the lambda saw" and "a value someone changed afterward" — the captured value simply can't drift. And this is the same rule anonymous classes had, back in module two; lambdas just inherit it.

Now, there's a workaround, but it's one to avoid. You can smuggle mutation past the rule by capturing a mutable container instead of a plain variable — the reference stays final, while its contents change. So an int array of one element, sum, is effectively final as a reference; but inside a for-each, you do sum-at-zero plus-equals n, mutating its contents. It compiles — but it's a smell, because it's fighting the design. Mutating shared state from a lambda is exactly what functional style is trying to move you away from — and it's unsafe if the lambda runs on multiple threads. The idiomatic answer is almost always to compute a result instead — int total equals list dot stream, map-to-int, sum. Reach for atomic-integer or atomic-long only when you genuinely need shared mutable state across threads.

There's one place lambdas actually differ from the anonymous classes they replaced: the meaning of this. Inside a lambda, this refers to the enclosing instance — not to the lambda itself. In an anonymous class, this meant that inner object. So a lambda can call the enclosing object's methods and read its fields directly. And, importantly, fields are not subject to the effectively-final rule — only local variables are. A lambda may freely read, and even mutate, an instance field, because the field lives on the object, not on the vanishing stack frame.

So the takeaway. Lambdas capture surrounding local variables by value, and those locals must be effectively final — so you never reassign one you've captured. Fields are exempt, because they belong to the object, and this, inside a lambda, is the enclosing object. And when you feel the urge to mutate a captured local, that's usually a signal to restructure as a computation that returns a value — the very mindset the last two sections of this module lean into, starting with composing functions.
